window.STUDIO=window.STUDIO||{};window.STUDIO.LESSON_AUDIO={"L1":{"folder":"L1-reading-table-4","dur":166.761,"scenes":[{"t":"Follow the electrons","a":0.5,"b":18.622},{"t":"Two words, two jobs","a":20.122,"b":53.794},{"t":"Pause and predict","a":55.294,"b":86.12},{"t":"How Table 4 is laid out","a":87.62,"b":115.161},{"t":"Writing a half-reaction","a":116.661,"b":139.239},{"t":"The trap with the numbers","a":140.739,"b":153.764},{"t":"Your turn","a":155.264,"b":166.761}],"cues":[{"s":"Follow the electrons","a":0.5,"b":5.779,"x":"Every reaction in this module comes down to one question. Where do the electrons go?"},{"s":"Follow the electrons","a":6.378,"b":17.622,"x":"In a redox reaction, one substance loses electrons and another substance gains them. The electrons lost are exactly the electrons gained. One cannot happen without the other."},{"s":"Two words, two jobs","a":20.122,"b":25.226,"x":"Oxidation is the loss of electrons. Reduction is the gain of electrons."},{"s":"Two words, two jobs","a":25.826,"b":34.821,"x":"The substance that loses electrons is the reducing agent. It donates electrons, so it reduces something else, and it is itself oxidised."},{"s":"Two words, two jobs","a":35.421,"b":44.368,"x":"The substance that gains electrons is the oxidising agent. It accepts electrons, so it oxidises something else, and it is itself reduced."},{"s":"Two words, two jobs","a":44.968,"b":52.794,"x":"In this studio, blue always means oxidation and the reducing agent. Red always means reduction and the oxidising agent."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":55.294,"b":61.803,"x":"Zinc reacts with copper ions. Which species is the oxidising agent? Decide now."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":66.403,"b":74.372,"x":"It is the copper two plus ion. It accepts two electrons from the zinc, so it is reduced, and it is the oxidising agent."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":74.972,"b":85.12,"x":"Not zinc: zinc is oxidised, so zinc is the reducing agent. And not copper metal: copper is a product. Always name a reactant as the agent."},{"s":"How Table 4 is laid out","a":87.62,"b":91.971,"x":"Now open Table 4. Every row is a reduction half-reaction."},{"s":"How Table 4 is laid out","a":92.571,"b":103.339,"x":"On the left of each row is an oxidising agent. As you go down the table, the oxidising agents get stronger. Fluorine, at the bottom, is the strongest."},{"s":"How Table 4 is laid out","a":103.939,"b":114.161,"x":"On the right of each row is a reducing agent. As you go up the table, the reducing agents get stronger. Lithium, at the top, is the strongest."},{"s":"Writing a half-reaction","a":116.661,"b":124.201,"x":"To write a reduction, copy the whole row with a single arrow. Copper two plus, plus two electrons, gives copper."},{"s":"Writing a half-reaction","a":124.801,"b":134.217,"x":"To write an oxidation, write the whole row backwards. Zinc gives zinc two plus, plus two electrons. The electrons are now on the right."},{"s":"Writing a half-reaction","a":134.817,"b":138.239,"x":"Then check that the atoms and the charges balance on both sides."},{"s":"The trap with the numbers","a":140.739,"b":152.764,"x":"When you balance electrons, you may need to multiply a half-reaction by two or three. You never multiply the electrode potential. The value belongs to the half-cell, not to the number of electrons."},{"s":"Your turn","a":155.264,"b":166.761,"x":"Now open the half-reaction builder and write three half-reactions of your own. Copy the row for a reduction. Write it backwards for an oxidation. And keep following the electrons."}],"bytes":2669228},"L2":{"folder":"L2-will-it-react","dur":142.05,"scenes":[{"t":"One rule","a":0.5,"b":21.74},{"t":"Zinc in copper sulfate","a":23.24,"b":52.146},{"t":"Pause and predict","a":53.646,"b":82.322},{"t":"The sentence that earns the marks","a":83.822,"b":101.643},{"t":"The trap","a":103.143,"b":113.287},{"t":"Water is always there","a":114.787,"b":132.004},{"t":"Your turn","a":133.504,"b":142.05}],"cues":[{"s":"One rule","a":0.5,"b":4.993,"x":"Will two substances react? Table 4 answers it with one rule."},{"s":"One rule","a":5.593,"b":20.74,"x":"Under standard conditions, a reaction happens when the oxidising agent is lower in the table than the reducing agent. The electrons run downhill: from a stronger reducing agent, high on the right, to a stronger oxidising agent, low on the left."},{"s":"Zinc in copper sulfate","a":23.24,"b":26.061,"x":"A piece of zinc is placed in copper sulfate solution."},{"s":"Zinc in copper sulfate","a":26.661,"b":39.631,"x":"The oxidising agent is the copper two plus ion, at plus zero comma three four volts. Compare it with the oxidising agent in zinc's own row, the zinc two plus ion, at minus zero comma seven six volts."},{"s":"Zinc in copper sulfate","a":40.231,"b":51.146,"x":"Copper two plus is lower in the table, so it is the stronger oxidising agent. It oxidises the zinc. A reddish-brown layer of copper forms, and the blue colour fades."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":53.646,"b":62.13,"x":"Now a silver wire is placed in iron two sulfate solution. Will it react? Decide, and say why in one sentence."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":67.73,"b":81.322,"x":"No reaction. Iron two plus, at minus zero comma four four volts, is a weaker oxidising agent than silver plus, at plus zero comma eight zero volts. So iron two plus cannot oxidise silver."},{"s":"The sentence that earns the marks","a":83.822,"b":92.646,"x":"The memos want a sentence with named species and the words stronger or weaker. X is a stronger oxidising agent than Y, so X oxidises Z."},{"s":"The sentence that earns the marks","a":93.246,"b":100.643,"x":"Compare like with like: two oxidising agents, or two reducing agents. And use the agents the question names."},{"s":"The trap","a":103.143,"b":112.287,"x":"Saying that silver is less reactive names no agent and gives no strength. When a question says, use the relative strengths, that answer earns nothing."},{"s":"Water is always there","a":114.787,"b":131.004,"x":"Remember that every solution contains water. An iron nail in copper sulfate reduces the copper ions, not the water. The water row, at minus zero comma eight three volts, is above the iron row, so water is too weak an oxidising agent to react with iron."},{"s":"Your turn","a":133.504,"b":142.05,"x":"Open the test-tube lab in Predict mode. For each tube, decide yes or no, write your sentence, and only then watch what happens."}],"bytes":2273708},"L3":{"folder":"L3-the-galvanic-cell","dur":161.138,"scenes":[{"t":"Make the electrons use a wire","a":0.5,"b":20.173},{"t":"Pause and predict","a":21.673,"b":54.839},{"t":"The two half-reactions","a":56.339,"b":77.921},{"t":"Where the electrons go","a":79.421,"b":107.574},{"t":"Cell notation","a":109.074,"b":127.495},{"t":"How big is the push?","a":128.995,"b":151.648},{"t":"Your turn","a":153.148,"b":161.138}],"cues":[{"s":"Make the electrons use a wire","a":0.5,"b":7.937,"x":"When zinc sits in copper sulfate solution, the electrons jump straight from the zinc to the copper ions, and the energy is lost as heat."},{"s":"Make the electrons use a wire","a":8.537,"b":19.173,"x":"A galvanic cell keeps the two apart. Now the electrons can only get from the zinc to the copper ions by travelling through a wire. That flow of electrons is an electric current."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":21.673,"b":30.877,"x":"Here is a zinc electrode in zinc nitrate solution and a copper electrode in copper nitrate solution. Which electrode is the anode? Decide now."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":35.477,"b":50.053,"x":"The zinc electrode. Zinc, at minus zero comma seven six volts, is a stronger reducing agent than copper, at plus zero comma three four volts. So zinc is oxidised, and the anode is where oxidation takes place."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":50.653,"b":53.839,"x":"The half-cell with the lower electrode potential is always the anode."},{"s":"The two half-reactions","a":56.339,"b":60.366,"x":"At the anode, zinc gives zinc two plus, plus two electrons."},{"s":"The two half-reactions","a":60.966,"b":65.398,"x":"At the cathode, copper two plus, plus two electrons, gives copper."},{"s":"The two half-reactions","a":65.998,"b":76.921,"x":"Add them and the electrons cancel. Zinc plus copper two plus gives zinc two plus plus copper. Use a single arrow every time. A double arrow costs a mark."},{"s":"Where the electrons go","a":79.421,"b":89.825,"x":"Electrons leave the zinc anode and flow through the wire to the copper cathode. They never enter the solutions or the salt bridge. In there, the charge is carried by ions."},{"s":"Where the electrons go","a":90.425,"b":99.355,"x":"What would you see? The zinc electrode gets thinner. A reddish-brown layer of copper forms on the copper electrode, and the blue colour slowly fades."},{"s":"Where the electrons go","a":99.955,"b":106.574,"x":"Name the electrode, not the half-cell. The examiners did not accept the half-cell or the solution as the anode."},{"s":"Cell notation","a":109.074,"b":126.495,"x":"Cell notation writes the whole cell in one line. The anode goes on the left, the salt bridge is a double line in the middle, and the cathode goes on the right. Zinc, zinc two plus, double line, copper two plus, copper. No coefficients and no electrons."},{"s":"How big is the push?","a":128.995,"b":144.439,"x":"The emf of the cell is E theta cathode minus E theta anode. Zero comma three four, minus bracket minus zero comma seven six, gives one comma one zero volts. Positive, so the reaction is spontaneous under standard conditions."},{"s":"How big is the push?","a":145.039,"b":150.648,"x":"Write the formula, substitute with brackets round the negative value, and give the answer with its unit."},{"s":"Your turn","a":153.148,"b":161.138,"x":"Open the galvanic cell lab. Pick two half-cells, predict the anode and the direction of the electrons, then press play and check."}],"bytes":2579372},"L4":{"folder":"L4-the-salt-bridge","dur":143.131,"scenes":[{"t":"Take the bridge away","a":0.5,"b":13.489},{"t":"Charge builds up","a":14.989,"b":37.962},{"t":"Pause and predict","a":39.462,"b":72.465},{"t":"What the memo wants","a":73.965,"b":90.389},{"t":"The trap","a":91.889,"b":102.87},{"t":"Choosing the salt","a":104.37,"b":135.925},{"t":"Your turn","a":137.425,"b":143.131}],"cues":[{"s":"Take the bridge away","a":0.5,"b":5.791,"x":"Watch what happens when the salt bridge is lifted out. The current stops at once."},{"s":"Take the bridge away","a":6.391,"b":12.489,"x":"Why? The electrons were happy to travel through the wire. So what was the salt bridge doing?"},{"s":"Charge builds up","a":14.989,"b":22.136,"x":"In the zinc half-cell, zinc atoms keep forming zinc two plus ions. Positive charge builds up in that solution."},{"s":"Charge builds up","a":22.736,"b":31.74,"x":"In the copper half-cell, copper two plus ions are removed as copper forms. The negative nitrate ions are left behind, so negative charge builds up there."},{"s":"Charge builds up","a":32.34,"b":36.962,"x":"Very quickly, this build-up of charge opposes the flow, and the current stops."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":39.462,"b":47.203,"x":"The salt bridge contains potassium nitrate. Which ions move out of the bridge into the zinc half-cell? Decide now."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":51.803,"b":58.92,"x":"The nitrate ions. They are negative, so they move into the zinc half-cell, where positive charge is building up."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":59.52,"b":71.465,"x":"At the same time, potassium ions move into the copper half-cell, where copper two plus ions are being removed. And ions from each half-cell move into the bridge too. Ions move both ways."},{"s":"What the memo wants","a":73.965,"b":81.127,"x":"The salt bridge has two functions. It completes the circuit, and it maintains electrical neutrality in the half-cells."},{"s":"What the memo wants","a":81.727,"b":89.389,"x":"When you describe it, name the ions and name the half-cells. Saying that ions flow through the salt bridge earns nothing on its own."},{"s":"The trap","a":91.889,"b":101.87,"x":"Electrons never enter the salt bridge. They flow only through the wire and the electrodes. In the solutions and in the bridge, the charge is carried by ions."},{"s":"Choosing the salt","a":104.37,"b":112.797,"x":"The ions in the bridge must not react with either half-cell. Potassium nitrate is the usual choice, because nitrates do not form precipitates."},{"s":"Choosing the salt","a":113.397,"b":124.957,"x":"Potassium chloride cannot be used next to a silver half-cell. The chloride ions react with the silver ions to form a white precipitate of silver chloride, which removes silver ions from the cathode half-cell."},{"s":"Choosing the salt","a":125.557,"b":134.925,"x":"Next to a permanganate half-cell it fails for a different reason. Permanganate is a stronger oxidising agent than chlorine, so it oxidises the chloride ions."},{"s":"Your turn","a":137.425,"b":143.131,"x":"Open the galvanic cell lab, change the salt bridge, and watch what each choice does to the voltage."}],"bytes":2290988},"L5":{"folder":"L5-electrolysis-the-two-contests","dur":180.757,"scenes":[{"t":"Push the electrons the other way","a":0.5,"b":32.865},{"t":"Two contests","a":34.365,"b":53.962},{"t":"Water joins in","a":55.462,"b":75.088},{"t":"Pause and predict","a":76.588,"b":103.181},{"t":"The trap","a":104.681,"b":121.512},{"t":"Chloride: concentration matters","a":123.012,"b":151.529},{"t":"When the anode joins in","a":153.029,"b":170.886},{"t":"Your turn","a":172.386,"b":180.757}],"cues":[{"s":"Push the electrons the other way","a":0.5,"b":11.734,"x":"In a galvanic cell, the electrons flow by themselves. In an electrolytic cell, a power supply pushes them the other way. It forces a reaction that would not happen on its own."},{"s":"Push the electrons the other way","a":12.334,"b":22.062,"x":"The electrode joined to the negative terminal is the cathode, where reduction happens. The electrode joined to the positive terminal is the anode, where oxidation happens."},{"s":"Push the electrons the other way","a":22.662,"b":31.865,"x":"So in an electrolytic cell, the anode is positive. The signs change, but the jobs never do. Oxidation is always at the anode."},{"s":"Two contests","a":34.365,"b":36.351,"x":"Electrolysis is two contests."},{"s":"Two contests","a":36.951,"b":42.233,"x":"At the cathode, every oxidising agent present competes, and the strongest one is reduced."},{"s":"Two contests","a":42.833,"b":47.974,"x":"At the anode, every reducing agent present competes, and the strongest one is oxidised."},{"s":"Two contests","a":48.574,"b":52.962,"x":"In a solution, there is always one extra player in both contests: water."},{"s":"Water joins in","a":55.462,"b":62.415,"x":"At the cathode, water can be reduced to hydrogen gas and hydroxide ions, at minus zero comma eight three volts."},{"s":"Water joins in","a":63.015,"b":69.633,"x":"At the anode, water can be oxidised to oxygen gas and hydrogen ions, at plus one comma two three volts."},{"s":"Water joins in","a":70.233,"b":74.088,"x":"Compare each ion with water. The stronger one wins."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":76.588,"b":84.411,"x":"Dilute sodium chloride solution is electrolysed with graphite electrodes. What forms at the cathode? Decide now."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":89.011,"b":102.181,"x":"Hydrogen gas and hydroxide ions. Sodium ions, at minus two comma seven one volts, are a much weaker oxidising agent than water. So water is reduced, and hydrogen bubbles off."},{"s":"The trap","a":104.681,"b":120.512,"x":"Sodium ions are attracted to the cathode, but that does not mean they react. Attraction decides only which way the ions drift. Table 4 decides which species reacts. Sodium forms only from molten sodium chloride, where there is no water."},{"s":"Chloride: concentration matters","a":123.012,"b":137.692,"x":"At the anode, under standard conditions, water, at plus one comma two three volts, is a slightly stronger reducing agent than chloride, at plus one comma three six volts. So in dilute sodium chloride, oxygen forms."},{"s":"Chloride: concentration matters","a":138.292,"b":150.529,"x":"But in a concentrated chloride solution, the high concentration of chloride ions makes the oxidation of chloride predominant. Chlorine gas forms. That is why brine is used to make chlorine."},{"s":"When the anode joins in","a":153.029,"b":169.886,"x":"An anode that is not inert is in the anode contest too. Copper, at plus zero comma three four volts, is a stronger reducing agent than water. So a copper anode is oxidised itself, and dissolves. Platinum and graphite are the usual inert electrodes."},{"s":"Your turn","a":172.386,"b":180.757,"x":"Open the electrolysis lab in Predict mode. Choose an electrolyte, predict both products, then run it and read the two contests."}],"bytes":2893100},"L6":{"folder":"L6-counting-electrons-faraday","dur":154.876,"scenes":[{"t":"Count the electrons","a":0.5,"b":10.186},{"t":"The chain","a":11.686,"b":26.99},{"t":"Step one: the charge","a":28.49,"b":50.489},{"t":"Step two: moles of electrons","a":51.989,"b":68.058},{"t":"Pause and predict","a":69.558,"b":94.302},{"t":"Step three: the mass","a":95.802,"b":104.782},{"t":"The trap the examiners keep seeing","a":106.282,"b":122.735},{"t":"Running it backwards","a":124.235,"b":145.095},{"t":"Your turn","a":146.595,"b":154.876}],"cues":[{"s":"Count the electrons","a":0.5,"b":9.186,"x":"In electrolysis, the electrons that flow through the wire are the ones that make the product. Count the electrons, and you know how much product you made."},{"s":"The chain","a":11.686,"b":14.256,"x":"Every Faraday calculation follows the same chain."},{"s":"The chain","a":14.856,"b":25.99,"x":"Current and time give the charge. Charge gives moles of electrons. The half-reaction gives the ratio. Then moles of product, and finally the mass or the volume."},{"s":"Step one: the charge","a":28.49,"b":33.858,"x":"A current of two amperes flows for one thousand nine hundred and thirty seconds, depositing copper."},{"s":"Step one: the charge","a":34.458,"b":42.164,"x":"The charge is current times time. Two times one thousand nine hundred and thirty gives three thousand eight hundred and sixty coulombs."},{"s":"Step one: the charge","a":42.764,"b":49.489,"x":"The time must be in seconds. Minutes times sixty. Hours times three thousand six hundred."},{"s":"Step two: moles of electrons","a":51.989,"b":59.562,"x":"One mole of electrons carries ninety-six thousand five hundred coulombs. That is the Faraday constant, on your data sheet."},{"s":"Step two: moles of electrons","a":60.162,"b":67.058,"x":"Three thousand eight hundred and sixty divided by ninety-six thousand five hundred gives zero comma zero four moles of electrons."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":69.558,"b":80.656,"x":"The half-reaction is copper two plus, plus two electrons, gives copper. How many moles of copper do zero comma zero four moles of electrons deposit? Decide now."},{"s":"Pause and predict","a":85.256,"b":93.302,"x":"Zero comma zero two moles. Each copper atom needs two electrons, so the moles of copper are half the moles of electrons."},{"s":"Step three: the mass","a":95.802,"b":103.782,"x":"Mass is moles times molar mass. Zero comma zero two times sixty-three comma five gives one comma two seven grams of copper."},{"s":"The trap the examiners keep seeing","a":106.282,"b":121.735,"x":"The most common mistake is to treat moles of electrons as moles of product. The examiners' report for twenty twenty-five said many candidates are still neglecting the ratio in the half-reaction. Always write the half-reaction first. It gives you the ratio."},{"s":"Running it backwards","a":124.235,"b":144.095,"x":"You can run the chain backwards too. To deposit fifteen comma nine grams of copper with a current of twenty amperes: find the moles of copper, double them for the electrons, multiply by the Faraday constant to get the charge, and divide by the current. It takes about two thousand four hundred and sixteen seconds, roughly forty minutes."},{"s":"Your turn","a":146.595,"b":154.876,"x":"Open the Faraday machine. Change the current and the time, switch to two cells in series, and watch both cells share the same charge."}],"bytes":2479148}};
